Market Challenges
FPR2 has complex ligand recognition and signaling behavior. Depending on the ligand and cellular environment, receptor activation may produce different biological responses. This complexity makes it difficult to predict outcomes and develop compounds with consistent, selective activity. Distinguishing FPR2 activity from that of related receptors, including FPR1, adds another layer of research difficulty.

Market Restraints
The development of FPR2-targeted therapeutics involves substantial research investment, lengthy validation processes and regulatory uncertainty. Pharmaceutical companies must establish safety, selectivity and efficacy before advancing candidates through clinical development. Limited commercial validation of earlier anti-inflammatory targets can also influence investment decisions and slow development activity.

